A submodule pin can move by accident, and nothing will tell you

One gotcha added to `AGENTS.md`, from a real regression in this repo rather than a hypothetical.

## What happened

A stale submodule checkout shows in `git status` as a modified path. So any broad `git add -A` or `git commit -a` sweeps the **checkout** into the commit as a new pin — inside a commit about something else.

`803812b`, a commit recording a proto-issue, silently rolled `journal/autumn-ryan` **backwards** three commits. That reverted #104 and un-published the correction to a named third party's quote which had already been merged upstream. Restored in #118.

**Nothing failed and no test caught it**, because a moved pin is a perfectly valid commit. That is what makes it worth a written warning rather than a fix.

## The reading trap underneath it

Three sources give three different answers to "what is the pin", and picking the wrong one is how this stayed invisible for a day:

| you ask | you get |
| --- | --- |
| `git submodule status` | the **checked-out** SHA; a leading `+` means it disagrees with the pin |
| `git ls-tree HEAD <path>` | the pin **as your local branch records it** — wrong if you are behind |
| `git ls-tree origin/main <path>` | the pin **as published**. This is the one that matters |

A sibling session's handover reported this repo's engine as un-adopted and gating the calendar work, because it read the printed SHA from `submodule status` as the pin. It wasn't — `.journal-engine` records current engine main on `origin/main` and nothing is gated. Same misreading, opposite direction, on the same day.

## Scope

Docs only, one bullet plus two sub-points, in the existing "Where intuition goes wrong here" section. No behaviour change.

Verified across `~/Project`: exactly two drifted checkouts exist, both in this repo, none elsewhere — so this is a real class of bug but not currently a spreading one.
